<h2>Grind Size Numbers for Popular Grinders</h2>

<p>
  "Medium-fine" means nothing until it's a number on <em>your</em> grinder. These are starting ranges in clicks or dial settings, counted from zero. Pick the middle of the range for your method, brew once, and then adjust by taste using the chart below.
</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table class="settings-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Grinder</th>
        <th>Espresso</th>
        <th>AeroPress</th>
        <th>V60 / pour-over</th>
        <th>French press</th>
        <th>Cold brew</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Comandante C40</strong><span>clicks</span></td>
        <td>7–13</td><td>12–24</td><td>18–28</td><td>26–36</td><td>30–40</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Timemore C2</strong><span>clicks</span></td>
        <td>6–12 <em>(tight)</em></td><td>11–20</td><td>13–22</td><td>22–30</td><td>26–30</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Timemore C3</strong><span>clicks</span></td>
        <td>5–10 <em>(tight)</em></td><td>9–18</td><td>11–18</td><td>19–25</td><td>22–25</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Baratza Encore</strong><span>dial 0–40</span></td>
        <td>Not ideal</td><td>8–18</td><td>12–18</td><td>24–32</td><td>28–40</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Fellow Ode Gen 2</strong><span>dial 1–11</span></td>
        <td>Not designed for it</td><td>3–5</td><td>4–7</td><td>8–10</td><td>9–11</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p class="table-note">
  <strong>Find your zero first.</strong> With the grinder empty, close the burrs gently until they just touch. That's zero, and you count from there. If your zero sits a few clicks away from the one a chart assumes, every range shifts by that amount, which is why two people with the same grinder can swear by different numbers. Once a setting works, log it in Brewio with the bean so you never have to find it twice.
</p>

<h2>The Relationship: Grind ↔ Contact Time</h2>

<p>Here's the principle that makes grind size intuitive:</p>

<div class="principle-card">
  <div class="pc-rule">Short contact time → Fine grind</div>
  <div class="pc-rule">Long contact time → Coarse grind</div>
  <div class="pc-explain">
    Espresso forces water through grounds in 25 seconds, so the grind must be very fine to extract enough flavour in that tiny window. Cold brew steeps for 16 hours, so the grind must be very coarse to avoid over-extracting during all that time.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Coffee Grind Taste Chart: Sour, Bitter or Weak?</h2>

<p>Your cup tells you which way to turn the grinder. Taste, then adjust one step at a time, about 2 clicks on a hand grinder:</p>

<div class="feedback-grid">
  <div class="fb-card fb-card--sour">
    <div class="fb-taste">Sour / Thin / Weak</div>
    <div class="fb-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="fb-action">Grind <strong>finer</strong></div>
    <p><a href="/guides/extraction-yield-guide/">Under-extracted</a>. Water passed through too quickly or didn't find enough surface area.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fb-card fb-card--good">
    <div class="fb-taste">Sweet / Balanced / Clean</div>
    <div class="fb-arrow">✓</div>
    <div class="fb-action"><strong>You're dialed in</strong></div>
    <p>Log this grind setting so you can reproduce it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fb-card fb-card--bitter">
    <div class="fb-taste">Bitter / Dry / Harsh</div>
    <div class="fb-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="fb-action">Grind <strong>coarser</strong></div>
    <p>Over-extracted. Too much surface area, or contact time was too long for this grind.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table class="settings-table taste-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>If it tastes…</th><th>What's happening</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Sour, sharp, salty, thin</strong></td><td>Under-extracted: water ran through too fast</td><td>Grind <strong>finer</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Bitter, harsh, drying</strong></td><td>Over-extracted: too much surface area or contact time</td><td>Grind <strong>coarser</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Weak or watery, but not sour</strong></td><td>Usually too little coffee, not the grind</td><td>Check your <a href="/guides/coffee-to-water-ratio/">ratio</a> first</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sour <em>and</em> bitter at once</strong></td><td>Uneven extraction: channeling, or a grinder making dust and boulders</td><td>Pour more evenly; a burr grinder helps most</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Muddy or silty</strong></td><td>Too fine for the filter (common in French press)</td><td>Grind <strong>coarser</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2>Burr vs. Blade Grinder: Does It Matter?</h2>

<p><strong>Yes, a lot.</strong> This is the most important equipment upgrade you can make, more impactful than a better brewer or fancier beans.</p>

<div class="grinder-compare">
  <div class="grn-card">
    <h3>Blade Grinder</h3>
    <div class="grn-verdict grn-verdict--bad">Not recommended</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Chops beans randomly into a mix of dust and boulders</li>
      <li>Inconsistent particle size = uneven extraction</li>
      <li>Some grounds over-extract while others under-extract simultaneously</li>
      <li>No way to adjust grind size precisely</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="grn-card">
    <h3>Burr Grinder</h3>
    <div class="grn-verdict grn-verdict--good">Worth the investment</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Crushes beans between two burrs into even particles</li>
      <li>Consistent size = even extraction = better flavour</li>
      <li>Adjustable from Turkish to cold brew</li>
      <li>Even a $40 hand burr grinder outperforms a $100 blade grinder</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

